#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198202 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b3101aa-ee9e-5940-b170-2f4e78802150" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Divisibility of a Product"}] :question/id #uuid "c3086c7e-3018-5071-a7ad-cc49f139685e" :question/math-academy-id "e-13317" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $3^3 \\: | \\: 3^2b$ then $3^3 \\: | \\: b$\n\n- If $20 \\: | \\: 30b$ then $20 \\: | \\: b$\n\n- If $2^5 \\: | \\: 3^4b$ then $2^5 \\: | \\: b$" :question/worked-solution "For any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$\n\n$c \\:|\\: ab \\quad$ and $\\quad \\text{gcd}(a,c)=1$ $\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad$ $\\quad c \\:|\\: b.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, let ${\\color{blue}{b}}={\\color{blue}{3}}.$ Then, $3^3\\mid 3^2\\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}}$ but $3^3\\not\\mid {\\color{blue}{3}}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, let ${\\color{blue}{b}}={\\color{blue}{2}}.$ Then, $20\\mid 30\\cdot {\\color{blue}{2}}$ but $20\\not\\mid {\\color{blue}{2}}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(2^5, 3^4)=1.$ Therefore, we have $2^5 \\: | \\: 3^4b$ and $\\text{gcd}(2^5, 3^4)=1,$ which implies $2^5\\: | \\: b.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198203 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198311 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c37bf66e-a574-5cf1-9c1a-fdfda98ca27b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Divisibility by a Product"}] :question/id #uuid "5faa797e-0bc2-53b3-875e-6d26b82d7074" :question/math-academy-id "e-13318" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $3 \\: | \\: a$ and $21 \\: | \\: a$ then $63 \\: | \\: a$\n\n- If $3 \\: | \\: b$ and $22 \\: | \\: b$ then $66 \\: | \\: b$\n\n- If $2^3 \\: | \\: c$ and $3^2 \\: | \\: c$ then $72 \\: | \\: c$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$\n\n$a \\:|\\: c,$ $\\quad b \\:|\\: c, \\quad$ and $\\quad \\text{gcd}(a,b)=1$ $\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad$ $ab \\:|\\: c.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, let ${\\color{blue}{a}}={\\color{blue}{21}}.$ Then, $(3\\mid {\\color{blue}{21}})$ and $(21\\mid {\\color{blue}{21}}),$ but $(63\\not\\mid {\\color{blue}{21}}).$\n\n- Statement II is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(3, 22)=1$ and $3\\cdot 22 = 66.$ Therefore, we have $(3 \\: | \\: b), (22 \\: | \\: b),$ and $\\text{gcd}(3, 22)=1,$ which implies $(66\\: | \\: b).$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(2^3, 3^2)=1$ and $2^3\\cdot 3^2 = 72.$ Therefore, we have $(2^3 \\: | \\: c), (3^2 \\: | \\: c),$ and $\\text{gcd}(2^3, 3^2)=1,$ which implies $(72 \\: | \\: c).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198207 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198309 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb430d5-5241-56a2-96be-73f44b0f2114" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Transitivity of Divisibility"}] :question/id #uuid "e16c9c14-7461-51b3-b16a-e62e76527719" :question/math-academy-id "e-13322" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $5 \\mid m$ then $25 \\mid m$\n\n- If $25 \\mid m$ then $10 \\mid m$\n\n- If $25 \\mid m$ then $5 \\mid m$" :question/worked-solution "For any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$ we have transitivity of divisibility:\n\n$a \\mid b \\quad$ and $\\quad b \\mid c$ $\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad$ $a \\mid c$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, let ${\\color{blue}{m}}={\\color{blue}{5}}.$ Then, $5\\mid {\\color{blue}{5}}$ but $25\\not \\mid {\\color{blue}{5}}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, let ${\\color{blue}{m}}={\\color{blue}{25}}.$ Then, $25\\mid {\\color{blue}{25}}$ but $10\\not \\mid {\\color{blue}{25}}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, note that $5 \\mid 25.$ Therefore, we have\n$5\\mid 25$ and $25\\mid m,$ which implies that $5 \\mid m.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198890 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198311 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c37bf66e-a574-5cf1-9c1a-fdfda98ca27b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Divisibility by a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab69d63a-d389-5177-85b1-c6e6932c4190" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f∣h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186196774} {:answer/id #uuid "e3fa3fde-9830-5592-a753-73393f9285a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f∣d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186196775} {:answer/id #uuid "5d4fa7d7-c92c-5ca1-8222-47512dd08552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f∣dh" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186196776} {:answer/id #uuid "6bba709c-96fc-5072-bc2d-4f563377f8c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "dh∣f" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186196777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6bba709c-96fc-5072-bc2d-4f563377f8c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "dh∣f" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186196777} :answer-field/id #uuid "992c74e6-3302-5a5a-be16-df1d99d267a8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186196773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e39353be-ada1-5677-a4dc-726e25573204" :question/math-academy-id "q-184523" :question/problem "Let $d,h,$ and $f$ be integers. If $d∣f,$ $h∣f,$ 