:answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197945} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f973ddc-35f8-5608-8069-a0955b9994f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f6ff943b-6246-50f5-830d-737c87bafa4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-92248" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $15\\,|\\,32b$ then $15\\,|\\,b$\n- If ${7}^{2}\\,|\\,7b$ then ${7}^{2}\\,|\\,b$\n- If ${3}^{2}\\,|\\,25b$ then $9\\,|\\,b$" :question/worked-solution "For any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$\n\n$c\\,|\\,ab\\,$ and $\\,\\text{gcd}(a,c)=1$ $\\,⟹\\,$ $\\,c\\,|\\,b.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(15,32)=1.$ Therefore, we have $15\\,|\\,32b$ and $\\text{gcd}(15,32)=1,$ which implies $15\\,|\\,b.$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, let $b=7.$ Then, ${7}^{2}∣7\\cdot 7$ but ${7}^{2}∤7.$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}({3}^{2},25)=1.$ Therefore, we have ${3}^{2}∣25b$ and $\\text{gcd}({3}^{2},25)=1,$ which implies ${3}^{2}\\,|\\,b.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420269 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b3101aa-ee9e-5940-b170-2f4e78802150" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Divisibility of a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47123b5a-4d9e-5980-b7e8-1b9b31e1fa9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420201} {:answer/id #uuid "8d1afd65-b437-5472-b418-2dc1d6ebe865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420219} {:answer/id #uuid "a964c9b7-bccb-5c9b-b3c7-48de44c00f07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I,II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420224} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb9bd8f-1205-50c0-a72f-14df58d7f13a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420235} {:answer/id #uuid "e26579ec-ded1-5c35-9155-e69045a20a37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcb9bd8f-1205-50c0-a72f-14df58d7f13a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420235} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5e3230f-cb4f-51e8-a721-e24f62b0c3ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420252}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f3b9fb7f-2262-5e34-bb97-3a915acfa755" :question/math-academy-id "q-93648" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If ${3}^{2}\\,|\\,10b$ then ${3}^{2}\\,|\\,b$\n- If ${17}^{2}\\,|\\,17b$ then ${17}^{2}\\,|\\,b$\n- If $20\\,|\\,{3}^{3}b$ then $20\\,|\\,b$" :question/worked-solution "For any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$\n\n$c\\,|\\,ab\\,$ and $\\,\\text{gcd}(a,c)=1$ $\\,⟹\\,$ $\\,c\\,|\\,b.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}({3}^{2},10)=1.$ Therefore, we have ${3}^{2}\\,|\\,10b$ and $\\text{gcd}({3}^{2},10)=1,$ which implies ${3}^{2}\\,|\\,b.$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, let $b=17.$ Then, ${17}^{2}∣17\\cdot 17$ but ${17}^{2}∤17.$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(20,{3}^{3})=1.$ Therefore, we have $20\\,|\\,{3}^{3}b$ and $\\text{gcd}(20,{3}^{3})=1,$ which implies $20\\,|\\,b.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420260 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b3101aa-ee9e-5940-b170-2f4e78802150" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Divisibility of a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25938baa-529b-5f86-828c-2801671fe4d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420192} {:answer/id #uuid "3cabb55e-40d7-5b81-a1ac-1144923f9b49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420200} {:answer/id #uuid "550ce662-95ae-5c42-94f7-d6898e204e74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420205} {:answer/id #uuid "7760ef9f-a800-540a-87c6-3414de29afe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420216} {:answer/id #uuid "df951e81-7965-5468-a3e1-c106088a53a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df951e81-7965-5468-a3e1-c106088a53a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420238} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfb4bbf3-86cf-55ec-89a8-e2c06957f446" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42a248b1-4b2a-5a7f-b673-589ac28da9a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-93649" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $10\\,|\\,21b$ then $10\\,|\\,b$\n- If $21\\,|\\,25b$ then $21\\,|\\,b$\n- If $15\\,|\\,9b$ then $15\\,|\\,b$" :question/worked-solution "For any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$\n\n$c\\,|\\,ab\\,$ and $\\,\\text{gcd}(a,c)=1$ $\\,⟹\\,$ $\\,c\\,|\\,b.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(10,21)=1.$ Therefore, we have $10\\,|\\,21b$ and $\\text{gcd}(10,21)=1,$ which implies $10\\,|\\,b.$\n\n- Statement II is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(21,25)=1.$ Therefore, we have $21\\,|\\,25b$ and $\\text{gcd}(21,25)=1,$ which implies $21\\,|\\,b.$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, let $b=5.$ Then, $15∣9\\cdot 5$ but $15∤5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420261 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b3101aa-ee9e-5940-b170-2f4e78802150" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Divisibility of a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06c38ff4-4134-5851-a52f-d46fca4ccc12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420186} {:answer/id #uuid "142f0a78-ef1b-5c40-b87c-2e4e6e998e29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420190} {:answer/id #uuid "28a1ea30-a1c0-5800-8caf-4ea2604323dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420193} {:answer/id #uuid "5bd1e956-1cac-5094-a64b-65c5d077b526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420207} {:answer/id #uuid "afdca230-1900-5d05-8b30-94bc667faa97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06c38ff4-4134-5851-a52f-d46fca4ccc12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420186} :answer-field/id #uuid "25f31e32-b105-56ba-945b-18f1eba552b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "553ed68f-8e74-5dda-a8b3-cdd3e89c31cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-93650" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If ${19}^{2}\\,|\\,19b$ then ${19}^{2}\\,|\\,b$\n- If $12\\,|\\,35b$ then $12\\,|\\,b$\n- If $22\\,|\\,{7}^{2}b$ then $22\\,|\\,b$" :question/worked-solution "For any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$\n\n$c\\,|\\,ab\\,$ and $\\,\\text{gcd}(a,c)=1$ $\\,⟹\\,$ $\\,c\\,|\\,b.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, let $b=19.$ Then, ${19}^{2}∣19\\cdot 19$ but ${19}^{2}∤19.$\n\n- Statement II is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(12,35)=1.$ Therefore, we have $12\\,|\\,35b$ and $\\text{gcd}(12,35)=1,$ which implies $12\\,|\\,b.$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(22,{7}^{2})=1.$ Therefore, we have $22\\,|\\,{7}^{2}b$ and $\\text{gcd}(22,{7}^{2})=1,$ which implies $22\\,|\\,b.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420262 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198311 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c37bf66e-a574-5cf1-9c1a-fdfda98ca27b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Divisibility by a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "144822ca-c64b-5d44-988e-8d3f58310385" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420191} {:answer/id #uuid "6b662c83-9382-549d-b78f-d6ae45b36298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420214} {:answer/id #uuid "ae35d862-ca5e-5f85-a3fd-da77890fb2a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420225} {:answer/id #uuid "afc58b19-6e0c-5559-867b-c0befae68830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420226} {:answer/id #uuid "da7300a2-932d-5ccd-b80e-d390c021dccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da7300a2-932d-5ccd-b80e-d390c021dccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420234} :answer-field/id #uuid "f237d89a-f9be-5d52-a1df-add8fdc50be1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ddd359d-678b-53c8-9991-000fcf922944" :question/math-academy-id "q-93657" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $3\\,|\\,a$ and $2\\,|\\,a$ then $6\\,|\\,a$\n- If $7\\,|\\,b$ and $14\\,|\\,b$ then $98\\,|\\,b$\n- If ${2}^{3}\\,|\\,c$ and $3\\,|\\,c$ then $24\\,|\\,c$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$\n\n$a\\,|\\,c,$ $\\,b\\,|\\,c,\\,$ and $\\,\\text{gcd}(a,b)=1$ $\\,⟹\\,$ $ab\\,|\\,c$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(2,3)=1$ and $2\\cdot 3=6.$ Therefore, we have $(2\\,|\\,a),(3\\,|\\,a),$ and $\\text{gcd}(2,3)=1,$ which implies $(6\\,|\\,a).$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, let $b=14.$ Then, $(7∣14)$ and $(14∣14),$ but $(98∤14).$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}({2}^{3},3)=1$ and ${2}^{3}\\cdot 3=24.$ Therefore, we have $({2}^{3}\\,|\\,c),(3\\,|\\,c),$ and $\\text{gcd}({2}^{3},3)=1,$ which implies $(24\\,|\\,c).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420264 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198311 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c37bf66e-a574-5cf1-9c1a-fdfda98ca27b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Divisibility by a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cbae4e4-2423-5869-9afa-da82e1b33b03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420188} {:answer/id #uuid "63d2cb0f-4fa8-5c73-8c7f-8dfd3f5884fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420211} {:answer/id #uuid "9f28d17f-a614-54b1-8f45-4295cb2a9f09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420221} {:answer/id #uuid "c915309d-8abd-5446-a1f9-f241c7dc3116" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420228} {:answer/id #uuid "ec1d996a-1c8d-5142-913e-181d70bfe766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420240}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cbae4e4-2423-5869-9afa-da82e1b33b03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420188} :answer-field/id #uuid "9800a386-864d-5b89-9fa0-d8050a82fd2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d7153a0-178f-57d1-b332-d3ea8e076028" :question/math-academy-id "q-93658" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $4\\,|\\,a$ and $9\\,|\\,a$ then $36\\,|\\,a$\n- If $5\\,|\\,b$ and $7\\,|\\,b$ then $35\\,|\\,b$\n- If ${6}^{2}\\,|\\,c$ and $2\\,|\\,c$ then $72\\,|\\,c$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$\n\n$a\\,|\\,c,$ $\\,b\\,|\\,c,\\,$ and $\\,\\text{gcd}(a,b)=1$ $\\,⟹\\,$ $ab\\,|\\,c$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(4,9)=1$ and $4\\cdot 9=36.$ Therefore, we have $(4\\,|\\,a),(9\\,|\\,a),$ and $\\text{gcd}(4,9)=1,$ which implies $(36\\,|\\,a).$\n\n- Statement II is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(5,7)=1$ and $5\\cdot 7=35.$ Therefore, we have $(5\\,|\\,b),(7\\,|\\,b),$ and $\\text{gcd}(5,7)=1,$ which implies $(35\\,|\\,b).$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, let $c=36.$ Then, $({6}^{2}∣36)$ and $(2∣36),$ but $(72∤36).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}]}
